  "headword": "{日持|ひも}ち",
  "reading": "ひもち",
  "part_of_speech": "noun, suru verb",
  "gloss": "shelf life, keeping quality",
  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "shelf life, keeping quality, how well something keeps",
      "explanation": "How long food or other perishable items remain fresh or usable. Describes the ability of a product to stay in good condition over time."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "12886_himochi_ex1",
      "japanese": "このお{菓子|かし}は{日持|ひも}ちがする。",
      "english": "These sweets keep well.",
      "notes": null,
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "12886_himochi_ex2",
      "japanese": "{生|なま}クリームのケーキは{日持|ひも}ちしない。",
      "english": "Fresh cream cake doesn't keep long.",
      "notes": null,
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "12886_himochi_ex3",
      "japanese": "お{土産|みやげ}を{選|えら}ぶときは、{日持|ひも}ちするものにしたほうがいい。",
      "english": "When choosing souvenirs, it's better to pick things that keep well.",
      "notes": null,
      "has_audio": false,
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "USAGE:\nMost commonly used in the patterns {日持|ひも}ちがする (keeps well) and {日持|ひも}ちしない (doesn't keep well). Very practical everyday vocabulary, especially when discussing food gifts, souvenirs, and cooking. An important consideration in Japanese gift-giving culture, where {日持|ひも}ちするもの (long-lasting items) are preferred for souvenirs.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {日持|ひも}ちがする: to keep well, to have a long shelf life\n- {日持|ひも}ちしない: doesn't keep, perishable\n- {日持|ひも}ちする{食品|しょくひん}: food with a long shelf life",
